	<dependencies>
		<!--
		  - min 32: HaRP is the recommended deploy daemon from Nextcloud 32 on, and the
		  - Docker Socket Proxy it replaces is scheduled for removal in Nextcloud 35.
		  - max 34: 34 is what this app is tested against.
		-->
		<nextcloud min-version="32" max-version="34"/>
	</dependencies>
	<external-app>
		<docker-install>
			<!--
			  - The image is published. It lies on ghcr.io, it is pullable anonymously, and it
			  - is a real multi arch index with linux/amd64 and linux/arm64: measured against
			  - the registry API for 0.1.0 on 2026-08-19 and again for every release by the
			  - runbook in docs/store-submission.md.
			  -
			  - The tag follows the version, and that is not a convention: AppAPI pulls exactly
			  - the tag named here, so the version element above, this tag and the git tag
			  - have to be the same string. The release workflow refuses a tag push whose tag
			  - disagrees with the version element, and tests/unit/test_exapp_env_setup.py
			  - refuses a manifest whose image tag disagrees with it.
			  -
			  - Do not write the name of the version element with its angle brackets into a
			  - comment here: scripts/build_store_release.sh and .github/workflows/release.yml
			  - both read the version with a grep for that opening tag, and a comment that
			  - repeats it makes both of them read the comment instead.
			-->
			<registry>ghcr.io</registry>
			<image>street1983nk/mcp_connector</image>
			<image-tag>0.1.3</image-tag>
		</docker-install>
		<!--
		  - Exactly thirteen routes, and they are the whole external attack surface of this app.
		  -
		  - The three AppAPI lifecycle paths (/heartbeat, /init and /enabled) are deliberately
		  - absent, and this comment is the only place they appear here. A path that matches no
		  - route is answered with 404 by the proxy, while one broad route would make the PHP
		  - proxy reachable for all of them, and that proxy does not protect them: it attaches
		  - valid AppAPI headers itself, which turns the disable endpoint into a denial of
		  - service button for anyone on the internet (T-02-20). HaRP blocks those paths, the
		  - PHP proxy does not, so the narrow declaration is the control that has to hold.
		  -
		  - The fourth deliberately absent path is /purge, added in plan 05-06, and for it
		  - this is not defense in depth but the control itself. It is the handler of the
		  - occ command mcp_connector:purge, which ends every MCP connection of this
		  - instance: it hands every Nextcloud app password this app created back, empties
		  - its database and deletes its data key. AppAPI calls it over PublicFunctions,
		  - the same internal path the three above arrive on, so it needs no declaration to
		  - work. A declaration would make an irreversible, instance wide deletion callable
		  - by anyone who can reach the PHP proxy, for exactly the reason stated above: that
		  - proxy attaches valid AppAPI headers itself (T-05-26, pitfall 13 of
		  - 05-RESEARCH.md). The handler carries the same double check as the three
		  - lifecycle paths plus a mandatory force option, and a test asserts that this
		  - path appears in no <url> below.
		  -
		  - Order matters: the first route that matches path and verb wins, and its access
		  - level decides alone.
		  -
		  - No bruteforce_protection on the MCP route. The OAuth discovery flow of phase 3
		  - begins with a rejected request by specification, so a throttler armed on that
		  - status would lock out legitimate first connections (T-02-21).
		  -
		  - headers_to_exclude lists exactly the headers the proxy sets itself. An empty
		  - list let a client send its own AUTHORIZATION-APP-API next to the one the proxy
		  - attached, and which of the two a reader sees then depends on whether the proxy
		  - prepends or appends. The app refuses such a request outright since WR-01; this
		  - list makes sure it never has to (WR-01, IN-01).
		  -
		  - /mcp is PUBLIC since plan 03-01, and that is a deliberate trade, not a
		  - relaxation. The OAuth discovery flow begins with a 401 that carries the
		  - resource_metadata pointer, and that answer has to come from this app: with
		  - access_level USER, HaRP rejects an anonymous caller before our code sees it, and
		  - the client never learns where the metadata lives (spike open item, pitfall 6).
		  - The protection that access level provided was replaced in the same change, not
		  - dropped: exapp/middleware.py now reads the user id of the AppAPI header, and an
		  - empty one has to pass an own bearer check. While no token verifier is configured
		  - that check refuses every bearer, so the route is closed by default (T-03-01).
		  -
		  - The three well-known routes replace the single ^/\.well-known/ of phase 2, which
		  - was the accepted risk AR-02-06: HaRP matches route patterns with re.match, which
		  - anchors at the start only, so that pattern published the whole well-known tree of
		  - this app, present and future. Every route below names one document and ends with
		  - $; without the end anchor a pattern would still cover its neighbours, for example
		  - /.well-known/openid-configuration.evil (pitfall 14). Their order follows the way a
		  - client walks the discovery: the protected resource document first, then the
		  - authorization server document at the path appended OpenID variant a client finds
		  - on its own, then at the RFC 8414 spelling the reverse proxy rules rewrite to.
		  -
		  - The two /connect routes are the browser onboarding of AUTH-02, added in plan
		  - 03-04. They are PUBLIC because the person using them is not necessarily signed
		  - in to Nextcloud at that moment, which is the whole point of the flow, and because
		  - HaRP answers an anonymous request to a USER route itself instead of letting this
		  - app show a page. They take nothing but one unguessable flow id and a form field
		  - that is either start or cancel: no credential, no redirect target, no client id.
		  - The sign in itself happens on Nextcloud's own pages, and no page below /connect
		  - has an input a user could type a password into.
		  -
		  - The four routes of the authorization server (plan 03-05) are PUBLIC for the
		  - reasons D-38 names, one per route:
		  -
		  - /authorize is a browser route and takes GET and POST, the two methods RFC 6749
		  - allows for it. It is PUBLIC and not USER although a person walks through it,
		  - because HaRP knows no sign in redirect: an anonymous request to a USER route is
		  - answered with a bare 403, and this page exists precisely to take a user who is
		  - not signed in yet to Nextcloud's own sign in.
		  -
		  - /token, /register and /revoke are machine routes without a user session at all.
		  - They carry their own controls: /token authenticates the client with the secret it
		  - was issued and verifies the PKCE verifier, /revoke authenticates the same way, and
		  - /register is the one endpoint that is open by design (D-35) and is bounded by the
		  - three switches of AUTH-07 instead. All three take POST only; the OPTIONS the SDK
		  - would also answer is not declared, because no browser calls them cross origin in
		  - this topology.
		  -
		  - /authorize/consent is the consent screen and the bridge into the Nextcloud sign
		  - in: it shows who is asking, hands the user over to Nextcloud's own pages and
		  - waits for the result. It is PUBLIC for the reason /authorize is, and it takes
		  - GET only. The page takes one unguessable flow id and the sign in address
		  - Nextcloud handed us, and it refuses to render an address that does not belong to
		  - this instance.
		  -
		  - /authorize/decide is the decision behind that screen (CR-01). It is the request
		  - that turns a finished sign in into an authorization code, and the flow id cannot
		  - authorise it: that value belongs to whoever started the flow, which in the OAuth
		  - path is the client and not the user, so a stranger could finish a sign in
		  - somebody else performed (the Login Flow v2 relay). What answers that is the
		  - Nextcloud account behind the browser, and the app compares it with the account
		  - whose sign in produced the authorization.
		  -
		  - It is PUBLIC anyway, because HaRP resolves that account on a PUBLIC route too:
		  - it signs every forwarded request with AUTHORIZATION-APP-API and writes the
		  - resolved account into it, empty when the caller sent no Nextcloud credential.
		  - Measured on this exact route against a real HaRP: a browser session cookie, an
		  - app password and an account password each arrive as the account they belong to,
		  - and an anonymous caller arrives as the empty string, which is_user() never
		  - accepts (docs/oauth-setup.md, "The CR-01 counter check").
		  -
		  - access_level USER was the first shape of this fix and it was withdrawn, because
		  - HaRP does not only answer an anonymous request to a USER route with 403, it also
		  - records that answer in a blacklist of its own: ten refusals from one address
		  - inside HP_BLACKLIST_WINDOW (300 seconds by default, HP_BLACKLIST_COUNT of 10)
		  - ban that address from the WHOLE ExApp, every route and every access level alike,
		  - with 502. Refusals are not an anomaly on this route, they are its normal
		  - traffic: the relay attempt itself, a browser whose session expired while the
		  - consent screen was open, a resubmitted form, and the negative probe
		  - scripts/oauth_flow_check.py sends on every run. So the access level meant to
		  - harden one route took the whole connector down for the caller instead, while the
		  - check that actually separates the two accounts stayed where it always was: in
		  - the app, because HaRP cannot make it. The relay attacker holds a valid Nextcloud
		  - account too, so "signed in" is not the question, "signed in as whom" is. Same
		  - trade the /mcp route above documents, for the same reason.
		  -
		  - /connections is the page of EXAPP-02, added in plan 04-03: a user sees the
		  - assistant apps connected to their own account, disconnects one of them, and
		  - pauses or resumes the MCP access of that account. GET lists and POST acts, and
		  - there is deliberately no DELETE: every state change of this page is a POST with a
		  - named action field and a hidden anti forgery value, which is also why the list,
		  - the confirmation, the disconnect and the switch are one route instead of four.
		  -
		  - PUBLIC for the measured reason of /authorize/decide above, and it is that reason
		  - twice: HaRP resolves the Nextcloud account of a request on a PUBLIC route too and
		  - writes it into AUTHORIZATION-APP-API, empty when the caller sent no credential,
		  - and this app refuses an empty one with a page of its own (403, "Sign in to see
		  - your connections"). With access_level USER, HaRP would answer such a request
		  - itself and record it in its blacklist: ten refusals from one address inside
		  - HP_BLACKLIST_WINDOW (300 seconds by default, HP_BLACKLIST_COUNT of 10) ban that
		  - address from the WHOLE ExApp, every route and every access level alike, with 502.
		  - A page reached from a stale tab or an expired session produces exactly those
		  - refusals as normal operation, so the level meant to harden this page would take
		  - the connector down for the person it belongs to. The check that actually decides
		  - stays where it has to be, in the app: it compares the account HaRP resolved with
		  - the owner of every row it shows or ends.
		-->
		<routes>
			<route>
				<url>^/mcp/?$</url>
				<verb>GET,POST,DELETE</verb>
				<access_level>PUBLIC</access_level>
				<headers_to_exclude>["AUTHORIZATION-APP-API","EX-APP-ID","EX-APP-VERSION","AA-VERSION","X-ORIGIN-IP"]</headers_to_exclude>
			</route>
			<route>
				<url>^/\.well-known/oauth-protected-resource/mcp$</url>
				<verb>GET</verb>
				<access_level>PUBLIC</access_level>
				<headers_to_exclude>["AUTHORIZATION-APP-API","EX-APP-ID","EX-APP-VERSION","AA-VERSION","X-ORIGIN-IP"]</headers_to_exclude>
			</route>
			<route>
				<url>^/\.well-known/openid-configuration$</url>
				<verb>GET</verb>
				<access_level>PUBLIC</access_level>
				<headers_to_exclude>["AUTHORIZATION-APP-API","EX-APP-ID","EX-APP-VERSION","AA-VERSION","X-ORIGIN-IP"]</headers_to_exclude>
			</route>
			<route>
				<url>^/\.well-known/oauth-authorization-server$</url>
				<verb>GET</verb>
				<access_level>PUBLIC</access_level>
				<headers_to_exclude>["AUTHORIZATION-APP-API","EX-APP-ID","EX-APP-VERSION","AA-VERSION","X-ORIGIN-IP"]</headers_to_exclude>
			</route>
			<route>
				<url>^/connect/wait/?$</url>
				<verb>GET</verb>
				<access_level>PUBLIC</access_level>
				<headers_to_exclude>["AUTHORIZATION-APP-API","EX-APP-ID","EX-APP-VERSION","AA-VERSION","X-ORIGIN-IP"]</headers_to_exclude>
			</route>
			<route>
				<url>^/connect/?$</url>
				<verb>GET,POST</verb>
				<access_level>PUBLIC</access_level>
				<headers_to_exclude>["AUTHORIZATION-APP-API","EX-APP-ID","EX-APP-VERSION","AA-VERSION","X-ORIGIN-IP"]</headers_to_exclude>
			</route>
			<route>
				<url>^/authorize/?$</url>
				<verb>GET,POST</verb>
				<access_level>PUBLIC</access_level>
				<headers_to_exclude>["AUTHORIZATION-APP-API","EX-APP-ID","EX-APP-VERSION","AA-VERSION","X-ORIGIN-IP"]</headers_to_exclude>
			</route>
			<route>
				<url>^/authorize/consent/?$</url>
				<verb>GET</verb>
				<access_level>PUBLIC</access_level>
				<headers_to_exclude>["AUTHORIZATION-APP-API","EX-APP-ID","EX-APP-VERSION","AA-VERSION","X-ORIGIN-IP"]</headers_to_exclude>
			</route>
			<route>
				<url>^/authorize/decide/?$</url>
				<verb>POST</verb>
				<access_level>PUBLIC</access_level>
				<headers_to_exclude>["AUTHORIZATION-APP-API","EX-APP-ID","EX-APP-VERSION","AA-VERSION","X-ORIGIN-IP"]</headers_to_exclude>
			</route>
			<route>
				<url>^/token/?$</url>
				<verb>POST</verb>
				<access_level>PUBLIC</access_level>
				<headers_to_exclude>["AUTHORIZATION-APP-API","EX-APP-ID","EX-APP-VERSION","AA-VERSION","X-ORIGIN-IP"]</headers_to_exclude>
			</route>
			<route>
				<url>^/register/?$</url>
				<verb>POST</verb>
				<access_level>PUBLIC</access_level>
				<headers_to_exclude>["AUTHORIZATION-APP-API","EX-APP-ID","EX-APP-VERSION","AA-VERSION","X-ORIGIN-IP"]</headers_to_exclude>
			</route>
			<route>
				<url>^/revoke/?$</url>
				<verb>POST</verb>
				<access_level>PUBLIC</access_level>
				<headers_to_exclude>["AUTHORIZATION-APP-API","EX-APP-ID","EX-APP-VERSION","AA-VERSION","X-ORIGIN-IP"]</headers_to_exclude>
			</route>
			<route>
				<url>^/connections/?$</url>
				<verb>GET,POST</verb>
				<access_level>PUBLIC</access_level>
				<headers_to_exclude>["AUTHORIZATION-APP-API","EX-APP-ID","EX-APP-VERSION","AA-VERSION","X-ORIGIN-IP"]</headers_to_exclude>
			</route>
		</routes>
		<!--
		  - The five variables an administrator may set, and the only way to set them: the
		  - deploy daemon injects a variable into the container if, and only if, the
		  - manifest declares it here (AppAPI ExAppService::getAppInfo, measured in plan
		  - 03-08). Without the declaration, the "env" option of `occ app_api:app:register`
		  - is accepted and silently dropped, and the app then runs on the defaults of
		  - src/mcp_connector/config.py and src/mcp_connector/oauth/registry.py.
		  -
		  - NC_MCP_PUBLIC_URL is the one that has to be set on every installation. The
		  - authorization server calls itself by it: it is the issuer of the metadata
		  - document, the resource of the protected resource document and the target of the
		  - resource_metadata pointer in every 401. Left empty, all three name the
		  - documented default http://127.0.0.1:8765 and no client can complete a
		  - connection (docs/oauth-setup.md).
		  -
		  - The three switches of AUTH-07 and the one of AUTH-08 are declared for the
		  - administrator who needs them, with their shipped defaults empty so nothing here
		  - overrides the code: AppAPI drops a variable with an empty value instead of
		  - exporting it blank. An empty <default> element is worse than none at all, because
		  - AppAPI 34.0.3 exports it as the string "Array" and the store answers the upload
		  - with a 500 (pitfall 3 of 05-RESEARCH.md, held by the variable gate of
		  - tests/unit/test_exapp_env_setup.py).
		-->
		<environment-variables>
			<variable>
				<name>NC_MCP_PUBLIC_URL</name>
				<display-name>Public URL of this app</display-name>
				<description>The address this app is reachable under from the internet, without a trailing slash, for example https://cloud.example.com/exapps/mcp_connector. Required for OAuth.</description>			</variable>
			<variable>
				<name>NC_MCP_OAUTH_DCR</name>
				<display-name>Allow clients to register themselves</display-name>
				<description>Dynamic client registration (RFC 7591). On unless set to off. Claude.ai and ChatGPT need it to connect without an administrator.</description>			</variable>
			<variable>
				<name>NC_MCP_OAUTH_CIMD</name>
				<display-name>Allow clients to identify by their own document</display-name>
				<description>Client ID Metadata Documents, the way of the MCP specification 2026-07-28: a client names the address of a metadata document it publishes itself instead of registering here. On unless set to off. Setting NC_MCP_OAUTH_DCR to off switches this way off as well, so a closed door cannot be walked around through the other spelling.</description>			</variable>
